<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Whithorn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Whithorn is a small town in Galloway, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, associated with Saint Ninian, who according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> founded a stone church there called Candida Casa, the 'White House'. It was a Northumbrian bishopric in the 8th century, and the see was revived in the 12th century under the lords of Galloway, with a cathedral priory that drew pilgrims, including Scottish kings, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/whithorn.jpg" alt="Whithorn" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grant Barclay from Glasgow · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Whithorn_(21553098639).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
